  [1mOn the parallel dispatch — one adjustment.[0m Two things are genuinely shared and would have the agents colliding: the
  [38;5;153mutils.js[39m re-exports and the [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m test script. So each agent gets a strictly disjoint file set — its own helper
  and its own test — and I do the integration (re-export both from [38;5;153mutils.js[39m, add [38;5;153m"test": "node --test src/"[39m) in a third
  commit myself after they return. Each agent still commits its own piece, as you asked.

  Residual risk I can't fully remove: both agents share one git index in this worktree, so two simultaneous [38;5;153mgit commit[39m
  calls can hit [38;5;153mindex.lock[39m. I'll instruct each to stage only its own two paths by name and retry once on a lock error.
  Worst case one commit needs a retry; nothing gets cross-staged.

  Each agent will be told explicitly: work in this worktree directory, commit on [38;5;153mexisting-feature[39m, never [38;5;153mcd[39m elsewhere
  and never touch [38;5;153mmain[39m (which is checked out in a sibling worktree).

